 ## Overview
-Brick Volume Addressing with If-Else is a method to compute memory offsets for accessing neighboring samples in a brick-structured volume, primarily for resampling or gradient computation [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently determining whether adjacent samples lie within the current brick or in a neighboring brick by using explicit conditional checks [S1, unofficial]. Unlike [VoxelGrid](2159-voxelgrid.md) which assumes a uniform linear layout, this method handles the discontinuity at brick boundaries by falling back to a more complex calculation only when necessary [S1, unofficial]. It differs from its sibling [Lookup Table Addressing for Resampling](3535-lookup-table-addressing-for-resampling.md) by using runtime `if-else` branches instead of precomputed lookup tables, which incurs pipeline flush penalties but avoids table memory overhead [S1, unofficial].
+Brick Volume Addressing with If-Else is a method to compute memory offsets for accessing neighboring samples in a brick-structured volume, primarily for resampling or gradient computation [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently determining whether adjacent samples lie within the current brick or in a neighboring brick by using explicit conditional checks [S1, unofficial]. Unlike sibling methods that assume a uniform linear layout, this method uses an `if-else` statement to test if needed data elements are within one brick; if true, it uses fast linear addressing, otherwise it falls back to costly address calculations [S1, unofficial]. It differs from its sibling [Lookup Table Addressing for Resampling](3535-lookup-table-addressing-for-resampling.md) by using runtime `if-else` branches instead of precomputed lookup tables, which incurs pipeline flush penalties but avoids table memory overhead [S1, unofficial].
 
